  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The variant cleanly carries signed opaque feed bytes and defers CAS interpretation to the payload pCID, which is good layering, but it does not standardize the corrupt-chunk rejection, local accounting, or retry behavior that this scenario needs.",
    "strengths": [
      "Makes CAS/body-reference interpretation a payload concern with an explicit pCID dispatch boundary.",
      "Deterministic CBOR plus mandatory signature slots preserve stable bytes for local verification and later review.",
      "Unknown-pCID store-and-forward supports sparse knowledge and avoids central registry dependence."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Does not itself define CID-mismatch rejection flow, retry policy, or peer-local accounting records.",
      "Audit of a corrupt chunk still depends on the payload handler; the envelope alone cannot explain the mismatch.",
      "The spec leaves hop-local evidence placement flexible across payloads, wrappers, and signature conventions.",
      "The specimen is still a draft and not frozen."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Implementers may over-read a valid envelope signature as proof that referenced chunk bytes are also valid.",
      "If chunk bytes travel out of band, the default signature coverage may not directly bind the bad bytes to the signed evidence.",
      "Local corruption evidence may fragment across different payload or wrapper conventions, weakening interoperability."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Where should peers record CID mismatch and retry decisions so evidence stays local but interoperable?",
      "Should signed payloads bind advertised chunk references or actual chunk bytes strongly enough to prove Mallory sent bad data?",
      "How should unknown sig_pCID cases be retained or forwarded without losing corrupt-chunk evidence?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
